WEBVTT

1
00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:18.876
One late autumn day, John hitched me to the high-wheeled cart.

2
00:00:18.877 --> 00:00:23.213
Then he and the squire climbed in and we drove out through the gate.

3
00:00:23.214 --> 00:00:27.010
The squire had to go to a certain town on business.

4
00:00:27.011 --> 00:00:32.698
The town was several hours away, but I didn't mind the work.

5
00:00:32.699 --> 00:00:37.338
The cart was light and easy to pull, and the weather was brisk and cool.

6
00:00:37.339 --> 00:00:41.748
It had rained a lot recently, so there were puddles everywhere.

7
00:00:41.749 --> 00:00:48.181
The wind blew fallen leaves across the road in colorful showers.

8
00:00:48.182 --> 00:00:52.335
Soon we reached the first toll bridge.

9
00:00:52.336 --> 00:00:55.237
The river was high because of all the rain.

10
00:00:55.238 --> 00:00:59.144
The flat wooden bridge was just a few inches above the water.

11
00:00:59.145 --> 00:01:01.847
The toll keeper stepped out of his hut.

12
00:01:01.848 --> 00:01:05.703
"The river's still rising," he told the squire and John.

13
00:01:05.704 --> 00:01:08.952
"I'm afraid it could be a bad night."

14
00:01:08.953 --> 00:01:11.593
The squire looked out at the bridge.

15
00:01:11.594 --> 00:01:15.955
"Your bridge has strong rails on both sides," he said.

16
00:01:15.956 --> 00:01:21.627
"I doubt you'll have any trouble here. But we'll be careful."

17
00:01:21.628 --> 00:01:26.023
We crossed the bridge and continued on our way.

18
00:01:26.024 --> 00:01:33.399
When we reached the town, John took me to a local stable to rest and eat while the squire went to take care of his business.

19
00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:40.713
By the time the squire reappeared, it was late afternoon. We started for home.

20
00:01:40.714 --> 00:01:47.826
"The wind is getting worse," John said as we drove out of town.

21
00:01:47.827 --> 00:01:51.869
"I don't think we've ever been out in such a terrible storm!"

22
00:01:51.870 --> 00:01:57.092
The road passed some fields and then entered a forest.

23
00:01:57.093 --> 00:02:03.640
The wind whipped the tree branches around overhead, making me a little nervous.

24
00:02:03.641 --> 00:02:07.187
The squire also sounded nervous as he spoke.

25
00:02:07.188 --> 00:02:10.683
"I wish we were out of this forest," he said.

26
00:02:10.684 --> 00:02:16.119
"Me too," John agreed. "I hope none of these branches fall on us."

27
00:02:16.120 --> 00:02:20.618
Just as he spoke, there was a loud crack above us.

28
00:02:20.619 --> 00:02:27.347
A second later a large limb crashed down onto the road ahead, blocking our way.

29
00:02:27.348 --> 00:02:33.026
The noise and surprise startled me terribly. I stopped short and trembled.

30
00:02:33.027 --> 00:02:36.345
Many horses would have spun around and run away.

31
00:02:36.346 --> 00:02:41.033
But I had learned not to do that, and so I stood as still as I could.

32
00:02:41.034 --> 00:02:44.141
John jumped out and hurried to my head.

33
00:02:44.142 --> 00:02:50.952
"Good boy," he said. "It's okay, there's nothing to fear. It missed us."

34
00:02:50.953 --> 00:02:54.039
"Yes, but barely," the squire said.

35
00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:58.055
"And now we can't go on. What should we do?"

36
00:02:58.056 --> 00:03:04.542
They decided we would have to go back to the last crossroads and take a different way home.

37
00:03:04.543 --> 00:03:09.331
We did just that, but it took a long time.

38
00:03:09.332 --> 00:03:13.490
By the time we reached the toll bridge again, it was almost dark.

39
00:03:13.491 --> 00:03:16.438
We approached the bridge at a steady walk.

40
00:03:16.439 --> 00:03:20.543
But as soon as my feet touched the wooden boards, I stopped short.

41
00:03:20.544 --> 00:03:23.629
I could feel that there was something different about the bridge.

42
00:03:23.630 --> 00:03:26.316
Something was wrong.

43
00:03:26.317 --> 00:03:29.151
"Go on, Beauty," called the squire.

44
00:03:29.152 --> 00:03:32.412
He flicked the whip lightly to urge me forward.

45
00:03:32.413 --> 00:03:36.256
But the bridge felt so strange, I stayed where I was.

46
00:03:36.257 --> 00:03:39.564
The squire flicked the whip again.

47
00:03:39.565 --> 00:03:43.979
It stung me a little harder this time, making me jump in place.

48
00:03:43.980 --> 00:03:48.402
Still I didn't dare take a step farther onto the bridge.

49
00:03:48.403 --> 00:03:53.207
"Wait, sir," John said. "Maybe there's something wrong."

50
00:03:53.208 --> 00:03:56.778
He jumped out of the cart and walked up to my head.

51
00:03:56.779 --> 00:04:02.415
"What's the matter, Beauty?" he asked in his soothing way. "Come on, let's go."

52
00:04:02.416 --> 00:04:06.925
At that moment the toll keeper burst out of his hut on the far side of the river.

53
00:04:06.926 --> 00:04:10.918
He waved a light around over his head and let out a shout.

54
00:04:10.919 --> 00:04:15.841
"Stop, stop!" he cried. "The bridge is broken in the middle.

55
00:04:15.842 --> 00:04:19.737
If you try to cross, you'll end up in the river!"

56
00:04:19.738 --> 00:04:25.555
"Oh my!" the squire exclaimed. "What a close call!"

57
00:04:25.556 --> 00:04:27.689
"Good boy," John murmured to me.

58
00:04:27.690 --> 00:04:31.446
He took me by the bridle and gently turned me around.

59
00:04:31.447 --> 00:04:37.862
Then he climbed back into the cart, and we continued down the river to the next bridge.

60
00:04:37.863 --> 00:04:42.832
It was built much higher over the water, and we crossed safely.

61
00:04:42.833 --> 00:04:47.586
When we finally arrived home, it was well after dark.

62
00:04:47.587 --> 00:04:52.316
We found the mistress waiting up for us despite the late hour.

63
00:04:52.317 --> 00:04:58.364
"Are you safe?" she cried, hurrying out to greet us. "I was so worried!"

64
00:04:58.365 --> 00:05:01.312
"Yes, my dear," the squire said.

65
00:05:01.313 --> 00:05:05.881
"We are safe. But only thanks to our fine Black Beauty.

66
00:05:05.882 --> 00:05:12.198
If he hadn't been wiser than John and me, we all would have been swept into the river and drowned."

67
00:05:12.199 --> 00:05:16.023
He went into the house so I couldn't hear any more of what he said.

68
00:05:16.024 --> 00:05:20.291
But I was so tired after the long journey, I didn't mind.

69
00:05:20.292 --> 00:05:25.192
John took me to the stable and unhitched me.

70
00:05:25.193 --> 00:05:34.372
Then he gave me a warm mash with lots of extra carrots, along with extra pats and praise.
